{"name":"Hydro","hex":"#49747f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hydro","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Clipping of hydroelectric.","Clipping of hydroponic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Clipping of hydropower (“hydroelectric power”).","Clipping of hydroelectricity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hydro"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hydropower","blocks":[{"text":"Hydropower, also known as water power or water energy, is the use of falling or fast-running water to produce electricity or to power machines. This is achieved by converting the gravitational potential or kinetic energy of a water source to produce power. Hydropower is a method of sustainable energy production. Hydropower is now used principally for hydroelectric power generation, and is also applied as one half of an energy storage system known as pumped-storage hydroelectricity."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/The_Dam_%282890371280%29.jpg/330px-The_Dam_%282890371280%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydropower","wikidata":"Q170196"},{"title":"Hydroelectricity","blocks":[{"text":"Hydroelectricity, or hydroelectric power, is electricity generated from hydropower. Hydropower supplies 15% of the world's electricity, almost 4,210 TWh in 2023, which is more than all other renewable sources combined and also more than nuclear power. Hydropower can provide large amounts of low-carbon electricity on demand, making it a key element for creating secure and clean electricity supply systems. A hydroelectric power station that has a dam and reservoir is a flexible source, since the amount of electricity produced can be increased or decreased in seconds or minutes in response to varying electricity demand. Once a hydroelectric complex is constructed, it produces no direct waste, and almost always emits considerably less greenhouse gas than fossil fuel-powered energy plants. However, when constructed in lowland rainforest areas, where part of the forest is inundated, substantial amounts of greenhouse gases may be emitted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/ThreeGorgesDam-China2009.jpg/330px-ThreeGorgesDam-China2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroelectricity","wikidata":"Q80638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/El-v-01_ubt.jpeg/500px-El-v-01_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/El-v-01_ubt.jpeg/500px-El-v-01_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEl-v-01_ubt.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Higashiyama_Botanical_Garden_Shishiodoshi_20170617.gif/500px-Higashiyama_Botanical_Garden_Shishiodoshi_20170617.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Higashiyama_Botanical_Garden_Shishiodoshi_20170617.gif/500px-Higashiyama_Botanical_Garden_Shishiodoshi_20170617.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A shishi-odoshi powered by falling water breaks the quietness of a Japanese garden with the sound of a bamboo rocker arm hitting a rock.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHigashiyama_Botanical_Garden_Shishiodoshi_20170617.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg/500px-Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg/1280px-Braine-le-Ch%C3%A2teau_JPG02.jpg?utm